// Chapter 115's corpus: 47 sentences of plain prose from chapters 112, 113
// and 122 of this book, in book order (the repository's own text). Sentences
// with code, digits, symbols or non-ASCII characters were left out. The row
// index is the sentence's time; chapter 120's split assigns them.
TT_CORPUS :: [47]string.[
    "A small language model lets us run the complete experiment: define the data, train all parameters, measure held-out likelihood and generate a continuation.",
    "No text normalization or unknown ID is needed.",
    "This is a deliberately tiny CPU teaching model.",
    "It does not require a pretrained checkpoint or any GPU.",
    "IDs and whole-document partitions are fixed before training.",
    "Documents are joined with one LF and a final LF inside each split, so those separators are explicit training transitions; no transition crosses a partition.",
    "These small synthetic held-out results measure this experiment, not general language quality or real-world generalization.",
    "Subtract the row maximum before exponentiation.",
    "The loss is mean negative log probability over adjacent byte pairs; the first byte supplies context and has no prediction target.",
    "Let C[x,y] be the training transition counts, C[x] their row sum, and N the number of transitions.",
    "Normalization uses all transitions, including rows with few examples.",
    "One update changes every parameter in every observed row; unused rows retain zero logits.",
    "This is gradient training, not a count lookup substituted for an optimizer.",
    "The trainer starts at zero and performs the requested number of SGD updates with a fixed rate.",
    "It records the initial loss and every updated training loss.",
    "Admission finishes before training writes, all updates occur in scratch, and only the completed model and trace publish.",
    "The host adapter observes overlap across input and all writable spans.",
    "Arithmetic and policy stay in Coax.",
    "Callers retain valid aligned immutable input storage and exclusive output access for the synchronous call.",
    "It reports nats, bits and transition count.",
    "A model from the bounded zero-initialized trainer fits that parameter domain.",
    "Invalid shape, nonfinite data, bad rate, insufficient trace or actual aliases refuse.",
    "It uses the same word mapping as the existing gpt-oss sampler.",
    "Counter exhaustion refuses before emission; a zero-length generation preserves state.",
    "Categorical sampling scans IDs in increasing order with strict CDF comparisons; the final byte gets the floating residual and cutoff exactly one.",
    "Output publishes only after all requested draws.",
    "The example prints numeric bytes rather than interpreting them as text.",
    "The untrained portions of the alphabet still receive probability, so a small trained model can emit many non-text bytes.",
    "There is no claim of coherent prose from this one-byte context model.",
    "An independent stdlib Python oracle uses transition Counters and dictionary contexts, separate row copies for updates, and per-transition loss evaluation.",
    "Coax checks also compare analytic gradients with finite differences, known RNG words, strict CDF ties, all-byte endpoints, atomic refusals, trace tails and exact budgets.",
    "The compressed model is verification DATA, not shipped training logic.",
    "Updating provenance metadata did not rerun training: partition bytes and their hashes stayed fixed.",
    "Run the complete example: The transcript is exact for the named CPU build.",
    "The verification record records debug/release, mutation, native reference and sanitizer evidence.",
    "A decoder sees the whole prefix.",
    "This chapter builds the smallest complete one, runs it two ways (all positions at once, and one token at a time from a bounded key/value cache), derives every gradient by hand and checks each against a central difference.",
    "A token's embedding row is the first residual.",
    "Each block normalizes, attends, adds; normalizes, applies a feed-forward network, adds.",
    "A final RMSNorm and an untied vocabulary matrix give the logits.",
    "Saved values have a fixed place per block and position, so the forward pass can stop at any position and the backward pass knows where to look.",
    "Nothing at a later position is read, so a future token cannot change a past output.",
    "The checks run both paths on the same weights.",
    "Each decoded step's logits are bit-for-bit those of the full sequence.",
    "It builds the parameters by name and attends over the whole prefix for each position.",
    "The embedding row of each input token collects its first block's input gradient.",
    "Every parameter group of both blocks and the head receives gradient."
];
